1
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:07,320
The parcel was on the doormat when I came up from the street on Tuesday evening.

2
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,440
It was the first week of November, and it had rained since dawn.

3
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:15,966
The stairwell of the Greywell smelled of wet coats and boiled cabbage,

4
00:00:15,966 --> 00:00:17,580
as it always did in that month.

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:19,800
I live on the fourth floor.

6
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:21,020
There is no lift.

7
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:25,939
I had climbed all four flights with my umbrella dripping on every landing,

8
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:28,040
and I was in no humour for a puzzle.

9
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,206
The parcel was the size of a hatbox,

10
00:00:31,206 --> 00:00:34,380
wrapped in brown paper and tied with garden twine.

11
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:38,820
The knot was an old one, a sailor's knot, pulled very tight.

12
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,840
My name was on the label in ink: Miss A.

13
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:46,580
Farrow, Flat 4C, the Greywell, Calder Street.

14
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:49,560
There was no stamp and no postmark.

15
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,100
There was no sender.

16
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,400
I stood there a while with my key in my hand.

17
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,260
Then I carried it in.

18
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,360
I am a records clerk at the Records Office.

19
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:03,580
I spend my days checking that one piece of paper agrees with another.

20
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:06,220
So I did what I would have done at work.

21
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:10,820
I set the parcel on the kitchen table and looked for the order that explained it.

22
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:12,540
I had no order.

23
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,540
I had bought nothing in a month except stockings.

24
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:18,260
I cut the twine with the bread knife.

25
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,973
Under the paper was a box of plain deal, and under the lid, packed in shavings,

26
00:01:23,973 --> 00:01:25,000
was a television.

27
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:26,920
It was a small one.

28
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:31,400
I would say the screen was no wider than my two hands laid side by side.

29
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:35,054
The case was walnut, worn pale at the corners,

30
00:01:35,054 --> 00:01:37,760
and the glass was dark and slightly convex.

31
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:41,660
There were two brass knobs and no names on either.

32
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:43,700
I turned the set over.

33
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:47,120
There was no flex at the back and no socket for one.

34
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,100
I looked for a battery door and found none.

35
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:54,360
I said, aloud, "Well, that's no use to anybody."

36
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,240
My voice sounded foolish in the kitchen.

37
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,476
I put the set on the chest of drawers in my bedroom,

38
00:02:00,476 --> 00:02:02,380
because that was the only clear surface.

39
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:07,500
Then I ate my supper, washed the plate and read the evening paper to the end.

40
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:10,220
I did not think about the parcel again.

41
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:11,720
I want that understood.

42
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:13,500
I was not uneasy.

43
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:17,820
At ten I went round the flat and put out the lamps.

44
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,480
Kitchen first, then the sitting room.

45
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,580
Last, the little brass lamp beside my bed.

46
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:25,000
I lay down.

47
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:27,380
The rain was loud on the sash.

48
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,020
Something was wrong with the dark.

49
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,460
It took me a moment to see what.

50
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:37,680
It had a pale patch in it, low and square, on the far side of the room.

51
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:39,060
I sat up.

52
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:40,840
The television was lit.

53
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:42,340
There was no hum.

54
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:43,500
There was no click.

55
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:48,632
The glass glowed a dull silver, and in it, small and clear,

56
00:02:48,632 --> 00:02:50,820
a woman was climbing a stair.

57
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:53,540
She wore a coat and carried nothing.

58
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:56,820
She went up slowly, one hand on the rail.

59
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:01,080
At each landing she stopped and looked up the well, as if counting.

60
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:04,740
The rail was iron, with a brass cap at the turn.

61
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:08,478
The fourth step from the top was chipped on its nosing,

62
00:03:08,478 --> 00:03:13,129
and the wall beside it was painted the green that the landlord had chosen in 1949

63
00:03:13,129 --> 00:03:14,220
and never replaced.

64
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:16,620
I knew that stair.

65
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:19,260
I had climbed it that evening, with a dripping umbrella.

66
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,100
The woman reached my landing, and turned towards my door.

67
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,520
I did not open the door.

68
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:32,042
I sat on the edge of the bed and watched the woman on the glass stand outside it,

69
00:03:32,042 --> 00:03:33,820
one hand raised, not knocking.

70
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:37,420
Then the picture closed up to a point and was gone.

71
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:40,020
The room was only a room again.

72
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:43,380
I counted to a hundred before I put on the lamp.

73
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:48,926
On Wednesday I came home at six with a torch from the hall cupboard,

74
00:03:48,926 --> 00:03:50,720
a candle and a box of matches.

75
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:52,820
I meant to be methodical.

76
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:54,560
I am a records clerk.

77
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:57,600
It is what I do with anything that frightens me.

78
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:01,860
I turned out the lamps at ten, and lay with the torch beside me.

79
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:03,140
Nothing.

80
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,100
I lay there twenty minutes.

81
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:10,920
Then I switched the torch on, just to see my hand, and the set was already lit.

82
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:13,980
It had not waited for me to turn the torch off.

83
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:17,180
It had waited for me to stop looking at the room,

84
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:20,120
or the room had stopped being dark enough to forbid it.

85
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:21,400
I did not know which.

86
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:25,356
I switched the torch off, and the woman was on the stair again,

87
00:04:25,356 --> 00:04:26,800
a little further along.

88
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:32,200
On Thursday I lit the candle and put it on the sill, and the glass stayed blank.

89
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:34,720
I drew the curtain across the candle.

90
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:35,860
Still blank.

91
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:38,620
I blew it out, and the picture came.

92
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:41,320
I wrote the times in the back of my diary.

93
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:43,600
Wednesday ran four minutes.

94
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:45,480
Thursday ran seven.

95
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:50,703
On Friday I hung a blanket over the curtain rail, to be certain of the dark,

96
00:04:50,703 --> 00:04:53,380
and I sat up in bed with the diary on my knee.

97
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,260
It ran eleven minutes.

98
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:59,709
This time the stair gave way to a street, wet and lamplit,

99
00:04:59,709 --> 00:05:01,740
and I knew it by the third frame.

100
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:03,060
Calder Street.

101
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:06,241
The Greywell's own railings, then the chemist's,

102
00:05:06,241 --> 00:05:08,480
then the post box with the chipped crown.

103
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:12,840
Between the chemist's and the corner there was a shopfront I could not place.

104
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:14,320
A narrow window.

105
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:16,840
Gilt lettering, too small to read.

106
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:18,520
A blind half down.

107
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:20,540
Someone knocked at the door.

108
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:23,680
I put on the lamp, and the glass went out at once.

109
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:27,375
Stanley Ingram stood on the landing in his slippers,

110
00:05:27,375 --> 00:05:29,540
with his keys on their ring in his hand.

111
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:34,721
He is sixty or so, and slow, and he has kept the Greywell for as long as I have

112
00:05:34,721 --> 00:05:35,420
lived in it.

113
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:37,020
"Miss Farrow.

114
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:38,080
I'm sorry.

115
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,800
There's a noise coming through the ceiling of three.

116
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:42,100
A sort of hum."

117
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:44,320
"There's no hum," I said.

118
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:45,520
"It doesn't make one."

119
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,180
He looked past me at the bedroom door.

120
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:50,420
I do not know why I said it.

121
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:52,300
"I've been sent a television.

122
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:54,300
I never ordered it."

123
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:55,040
"A television.

124
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:56,380
He frowned.

125
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:57,680
"Let's have a look."

126
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:00,340
He stood in the doorway a long while.

127
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,100
He did not go near it.

128
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:05,160
"It isn't on the inventory," he said at last.

129
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:09,600
"I have the list of every set in this building, going back to the first owner.

130
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:12,020
Lodgers' wireless sets, the lot.

131
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:13,760
There's no walnut one.

132
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:14,860
And no plug."

133
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:16,100
"I know."

134
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:19,040
"Nobody's got one like that, Miss Farrow."

135
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:20,380
I made him sit down.

136
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:24,160
I told him what I had seen that night, leaving out the stair.

137
00:06:24,780 --> 00:06:26,853
I said only that it showed Calder Street,

138
00:06:26,853 --> 00:06:29,280
and a shop between the chemist's and the corner.

139
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:31,500
"Gilt lettering?" he said.

140
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,160
"Blind half down?"

141
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:34,480
"Yes."

142
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:37,200
He took off his cap and turned it round in his hands.

143
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:39,400
"That was Pell's, the clockmaker.

144
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:41,560
It shut in 1961.

145
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,480
It's been a bank for thirty years."

146
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:45,520
I looked at him.

147
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:48,080
"Which way was the blind?" he said.

148
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:51,700
"Which way was the blind?

149
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:54,060
Stanley had said, and I had told him.

150
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:55,960
Half down, I said.

151
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:00,320
Pulled to the left, as if someone had meant to finish the job and been called away.

152
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:03,800
He nodded slowly and put his cap back on.

153
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:07,180
He said he would sleep better not knowing why I asked.

154
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:11,580
Then he went down the stair, and I heard him stop twice on the way.

155
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:14,140
That was the fourth night.

156
00:07:14,860 --> 00:07:18,240
By the fifth I had stopped pretending it was a fault in the wiring.

157
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:23,782
On the fifth night I sat on the bedroom floor with my back against the wardrobe and

158
00:07:23,782 --> 00:07:27,180
the pencil in my hand, and I put out the last lamp myself.

159
00:07:28,420 --> 00:07:30,080
The glass woke at once.

160
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:34,824
The woman was on the stair again, the fourth-floor stair of the Greywell,

161
00:07:34,824 --> 00:07:37,080
with its brass rod missing at the turn.

162
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:41,760
She came down into the light of the landing window and I saw her face.

163
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:43,280
It was mine.

164
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:45,980
There was no doubt left to argue with.

165
00:07:46,540 --> 00:07:50,654
She wore the brown cardigan, the one with the leather button replaced by a black

166
00:07:50,654 --> 00:07:51,254
one.

167
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:53,700
I had worn it that morning to the Records Office.

168
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:56,800
I had hung it on the chair not two hours before.

169
00:07:57,620 --> 00:07:58,560
She carried a suitcase.

170
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,760
Her free hand held the rail.

171
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:04,320
She did not hurry, and she did not look back at the flat.

172
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:06,880
I have never owned a suitcase.

173
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:10,760
I had never once, in eleven years, thought of leaving.

174
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:15,520
I sat there until the picture went to snow, and then to black.

175
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:18,040
I put the pencil down on the boards.

176
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:20,830
I got up, found the telephone in the hall,

177
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:24,420
and rang a number I had to look for in the back of my address book.

178
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:27,540
Horace Trevelyan answered on the ninth ring.

179
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:31,700
He was eighty-one, and had handled the sale of the Greywell for the estate.

180
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:35,100
I had met him exactly once, at a signing.

181
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:37,360
"Miss Farrow," he said.

182
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:39,280
"It's gone midnight."

183
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:39,880
"I know.

184
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:40,760
I'm sorry.

185
00:08:41,180 --> 00:08:43,380
I need to ask about a television."

186
00:08:43,380 --> 00:08:45,300
There was a silence on the line.

187
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:48,900
It was not the silence of a man who had to think.

188
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:50,380
"Walnut," he said.

189
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,400
"Small.

190
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:52,100
No flex."

191
00:08:52,620 --> 00:08:53,220
"Yes."

192
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:55,140
"It came with the building.

193
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:59,120
It was never in the schedule of fixtures, and I was told to leave it off.

194
00:08:59,820 --> 00:09:00,820
He cleared his throat.

195
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:02,880
"The first owner left it.

196
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:04,120
A Mrs Ingram.

197
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:07,160
She lived on the fourth floor herself, as it happens.

198
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:11,040
She told me once that it was the only honest record of the tenants."

199
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:13,460
"The tenants," I said.

200
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:14,660
"Her word.

201
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:15,900
I took it for a joke."

202
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:17,560
"Mrs Ingram.

203
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:19,580
Any relation to the caretaker?"

204
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:21,080
"I couldn't say.

205
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:22,120
I never asked.

206
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:23,580
Another pause.

207
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,700
"She left in October of 1958.

208
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:31,440
Walked out one night with a suitcase, past the porter, who wished her good evening.

209
00:09:31,940 --> 00:09:32,940
She wished him the same.

210
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:35,000
Nobody saw her after that.

211
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:37,040
Her rent was paid, you understand.

212
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:38,640
Paid a good while ahead."

213
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:41,460
I looked down the hall at the bedroom door.

214
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:44,500
It stood open, and the room beyond it was dark.

215
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:48,820
"Mr Trevelyan, was there a woman living here after she left?

216
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:50,420
A woman who took her flat?"

217
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:52,320
He did not answer that.

218
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:53,800
I heard him breathing.

219
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:57,400
"Miss Farrow," he said at last, very gently.

220
00:09:58,060 --> 00:09:59,820
"Which floor did you say you were on?"

221
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:03,100
"Fourth," I said.

222
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:04,380
"The fourth floor."

223
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:07,080
"Yes," said Mr Trevelyan.

224
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:08,940
"That's what I was afraid of."

225
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:10,920
He would say nothing more.

226
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:15,380
He wished me goodnight, and he said it kindly, and the line went dead.

227
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:18,700
I stood in the hall with the receiver in my hand.

228
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:22,280
Then I put it down and went to the kitchen drawer for a pencil.

229
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,300
I found one, and a pad of the buff forms I brought home from the Records Office.

230
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:30,000
I carried them to the armchair in the sitting room.

231
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:32,080
I switched off the last lamp.

232
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:37,073
The television woke at once, as it always did, with that low grain of light,

233
00:10:37,073 --> 00:10:39,880
and I sat in front of it with the pad on my knee.

234
00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:42,040
I had decided on a method.

235
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:44,100
I would not watch the story.

236
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:49,000
I would copy it, frame by frame, as a clerk copies a register entry.

237
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:53,680
Time, place, who was present, what each person carried.

238
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:56,720
If it was a record, I could read it like one.

239
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:01,361
The woman on the screen came out of the front door of the Greywell at ten past six

240
00:11:01,361 --> 00:11:02,100
in the morning.

241
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:03,820
Calder Street was wet.

242
00:11:04,340 --> 00:11:05,040
She had a suitcase.

243
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:06,560
I wrote that down.

244
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:09,408
She turned left, toward the tram stop,

245
00:11:09,408 --> 00:11:12,260
and a man with a milk crate stepped aside for her.

246
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:16,760
Then the picture cut to the same door, the same hour, the next day.

247
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:19,220
A second woman came in from the street.

248
00:11:19,220 --> 00:11:21,180
She wore the brown cardigan.

249
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:26,969
She had my face, my walk, even the small hitch in the left shoulder where I carry my

250
00:11:26,969 --> 00:11:27,569
bag.

251
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:30,120
She nodded to the porter, and he nodded back.

252
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:31,600
He did not look twice.

253
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:34,300
I wrote: She is not followed.

254
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:35,840
She is not stopped.

255
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:37,600
The picture cut again.

256
00:11:38,140 --> 00:11:39,940
A different door, a different decade.

257
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:41,780
I knew it by the lamp-brackets.

258
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:46,132
A man in a belted overcoat carried a case out of the Greywell,

259
00:11:46,132 --> 00:11:50,280
and the next morning another man in a belted overcoat carried a case in.

260
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:51,680
The same face.

261
00:11:52,140 --> 00:11:52,900
The same coat.

262
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:55,380
A hat was lifted to the same porter.

263
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:57,960
I turned the page and kept writing.

264
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:00,300
A girl with a hatbox.

265
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:01,480
A widow, by her veiling.

266
00:12:02,140 --> 00:12:05,220
Each one went out at dawn, and each one was replaced.

267
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:07,160
Nobody remarked on it.

268
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:10,280
Nobody on that screen ever remarked on it.

269
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:12,080
My pencil had stopped.

270
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:15,940
I looked at what I had written, and it was not a forecast.

271
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:17,660
I saw that now.

272
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,240
A forecast is a thing you can refuse.

273
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:22,640
This was a ledger.

274
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:25,320
These people had not been going to leave.

275
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:29,640
They had left already, and the dark had only been showing me the entry.

276
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,120
I thought of Mrs Ingram, and her rent paid a good while ahead.

277
00:12:34,980 --> 00:12:38,560
On the screen, the woman in my cardigan was back in my sitting room.

278
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:40,380
I watched her cross it.

279
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:42,800
She sat in my armchair.

280
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,380
She put a pad on her knee, and she began to write.

281
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:49,900
I watched her write for nearly a minute, and I could not read the page.

282
00:12:50,540 --> 00:12:51,580
Then she stopped.

283
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:55,140
She lifted her head, and she looked out of the glass at me.

284
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:59,300
Slowly she turned and reached for the lamp on the side table.

285
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,520
Her hand closed on the switch.

286
00:13:02,220 --> 00:13:03,700
I knew what came next.

287
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:05,340
She would put the light on.

288
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:10,240
The set would go dark, and I would go to the bedroom and fetch my coat, and go.

289
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:12,520
I had been going to do it.

290
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:15,600
I could feel it, like a word on the tip of the tongue.

291
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:18,500
I did not move my hand toward the lamp.

292
00:13:18,940 --> 00:13:19,960
I stood up instead.

293
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:22,320
The pad slid to the carpet.

294
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:27,402
I crossed the room in the dark, with the picture throwing my own shadow ahead of me,

295
00:13:27,402 --> 00:13:29,160
and I took my coat from the chair.

296
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:33,380
It was the heavy tweed, the one I wear to the Records Office in winter.

297
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:35,800
I held it up with both hands.

298
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:40,240
On the screen, the woman in the cardigan was holding up a coat as well.

299
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:42,080
I laid it over the glass.

300
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:44,240
The light went out of the room.

301
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:49,701
There was no sound at all, and then, from beneath the tweed, very faintly,

302
00:13:49,701 --> 00:13:52,460
I heard something that was almost a knock.

303
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:56,920
I did not lift the coat until it was light.

304
00:13:57,400 --> 00:14:00,627
I sat on the floor with my back against the armchair,

305
00:14:00,627 --> 00:14:05,680
the tweed held down with both hands, and I let the knocking stop of its own accord.

306
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:07,520
It took some time.

307
00:14:08,380 --> 00:14:11,940
Towards five it grew weaker, like a clock running down.

308
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:17,040
After that there was only the rain on the window, and the sound of my own breath.

309
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:20,440
At eight, someone knocked at the door itself.

310
00:14:21,260 --> 00:14:25,380
It was a plain knock, two raps, the knock of a man with a clipboard.

311
00:14:26,060 --> 00:14:26,800
"Miss Farrow?

312
00:14:27,180 --> 00:14:28,080
It's Stanley Ingram."

313
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:30,340
I opened it on the chain.

314
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:35,080
He stood on the landing in his brown overall, with the rent book under his arm,

315
00:14:35,080 --> 00:14:38,560
and he looked pleased, as a man does who brings good news.

316
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:41,680
"Thought you'd want to hear it from me," he said.

317
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:43,180
"Your rent's been paid.

318
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:47,600
A full year in advance, Miss Farrow, in cash, to the office this morning.

319
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:49,840
I've never known the like."

320
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:51,700
"I haven't paid it," I said.

321
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:52,800
"No, no.

322
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:54,020
The lady did.

323
00:14:54,020 --> 00:14:54,760
He smiled.

324
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:56,260
"Brown cardigan.

325
00:14:56,620 --> 00:14:57,160
Very neat.

326
00:14:57,160 --> 00:15:00,760
She said you'd be tired, and I wasn't to disturb you till eight.

327
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:02,840
She left something for you in the hall.

328
00:15:03,180 --> 00:15:04,600
I've carried it up."

329
00:15:04,600 --> 00:15:05,500
He stepped aside.

330
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:09,240
On the landing, against the banister, stood a suitcase.

331
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:14,600
It was a leather case, old and square, with brass corners gone dull.

332
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:17,680
The strap was buckled once, not twice.

333
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:20,540
"Did you see her face, Mr Ingram?"

334
00:15:21,220 --> 00:15:22,660
He thought about it honestly.

335
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:24,400
That was the worst of it.

336
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:27,780
"Do you know," he said, "I couldn't tell you.

337
00:15:28,180 --> 00:15:29,240
But she knew the building.

338
00:15:29,700 --> 00:15:31,020
She never asked for the stairs."

339
00:15:31,780 --> 00:15:33,600
He went down, whistling a little.

340
00:15:34,360 --> 00:15:38,724
I heard him reach the third floor, and the second, and then the street door,

341
00:15:38,724 --> 00:15:39,700
and then nothing.

342
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:41,560
I took off the chain.

343
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:46,010
I knelt on the landing mat, where the parcel had lain in November,

344
00:15:46,010 --> 00:15:47,280
and I undid the strap.

345
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:49,080
The lid was stiff.

346
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:52,020
It came up with a small dry sound.

347
00:15:52,680 --> 00:15:56,620
The television lay inside, packed in my own blue scarf.

348
00:15:57,640 --> 00:15:59,120
The walnut case was warm.

349
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:02,150
I felt it through the wool before I touched it,

350
00:16:02,150 --> 00:16:04,400
as you feel a stone that has been in the sun.

351
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:07,770
It was ten past eight on a bright wet morning,

352
00:16:07,770 --> 00:16:11,880
with the skylight over the stair full of daylight, and the glass was lit.

353
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,720
The picture was faint but quite plain.

354
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:20,240
It showed a door, painted green, with a brass 4B above the letterbox.

355
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:21,840
It was my door.

356
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:26,700
It was empty on both sides, and the camera stood where I was kneeling now.

357
00:16:27,300 --> 00:16:28,440
The door was opening.

358
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:30,060
I turned my head.

359
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:34,160
Behind me, my own door stood shut, as I had left it.

360
00:16:34,740 --> 00:16:36,060
I looked back at the glass.

361
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:39,110
The gap widened, an inch at a time,

362
00:16:39,110 --> 00:16:43,888
and in the gap there was a hand on the edge of the wood, in a brown cuff,

363
00:16:43,888 --> 00:16:46,440
with a pencil held between two fingers.

364
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,820
I could hear the latch behind me begin to turn.

